  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms across western \nNY this evening. A few storms may be severe with strong winds and \nhail.\n\nLatest satellite and radar imagery shows widely scattered showers \nand thunderstorms across southern Ontario to west-central NY this \nevening. Mesoanalysis shows MLCAPE decreasing to less than 1000 J/kg \nwith effective shear 30-40knots. A shortwave trough will move across \nthe region this evening. Hi-res ensemble guidance (HREF/REFS) \nsuggest storm potential will continue this evening for the Niagara \nFrontier across to the northern Finger Lakes region. The western \nSouthern Tier may see storms remain in the area through midnight. \nLingering showers may remain into early Thursday morning before \nturning mostly dry throughout the remainder of the day as high \npressure begins to build into the upper Great Lakes.\n\nKey Message 2...Mostly dry weather late this week with showers and \nthunderstorms looking to return Sunday.\n\nAfter mostly dry weather Friday and Saturday, a low pressure system \nwill track across northern Ontario early Sunday with a trailing cold \nfront that may bring the next round of showers and thunderstorms to \nwestern and north-central NY. The timing of this low is still quite \nuncertain owning to the ~50% PoPs lasting throughout much of the \nday. While the severe convective environment appears better farther \nsouth and west, latest model trends has begun to shift the axis of \ngreater instability north into the western Southern Tier. \nAdditionally, lingering upstream convection from Saturday night \nacross the Midwest will be another factor. Afterwards, high \npressure will build back into the Great Lakes with drier weather\nfor early next week.",
